# Health foundation of ADR 0015's health amendment: reboot on fatal error,
# a hardware watchdog fed from evidence, and image self-confirmation.
# Public API: <mcuhome/health.h>.

menuconfig MCUHOME_HEALTH
	bool "MCUHome health foundation (fault reboot, watchdog, image confirm)"
	default y
	help
	  The three mechanisms an updatable device cannot go without: a fatal
	  error reboots instead of halting, a hardware watchdog resets a node
	  whose loops stopped, and a freshly swapped-in image confirms itself
	  to MCUboot only after it has been healthy for a while.

	  ADR 0015's health amendment makes the first two mandatory for every
	  application image, which is why this defaults to y. The fault
	  counters the same amendment specifies (fault-reset and long-run
	  counters filtered by hwinfo reset cause) are a later work item; what
	  is here is MCUboot's own test/confirm revert plus the watchdog that
	  makes a hung device reach it.

if MCUHOME_HEALTH

config MCUHOME_RESET_ON_FATAL_ERROR
	bool "Reboot on a fatal error instead of halting"
	default y
	select REBOOT
	help
	  Overrides Zephyr's weak k_sys_fatal_error_handler(), which halts the
	  system (kernel/fatal.c: LOG_PANIC() then arch_system_halt()). A
	  halted node is unreachable, unrecoverable without physical access,
	  and — worse for an updatable device — never reaches the boot in
	  which MCUboot would revert an unconfirmed image.

	  This is MCUHome code rather than a Kconfig assignment because
	  vanilla Zephyr has no CONFIG_RESET_ON_FATAL_ERROR; that symbol is
	  Nordic's, in the nRF Connect SDK. The behaviour it names is what
	  ADR 0015's health amendment requires, so MCUHome implements it.

	  Turn it off for a bench session where a debugger should catch the
	  fault in place. Never in a shipped image.

config MCUHOME_WATCHDOG
	bool "Hardware watchdog, fed from registered liveness slots"
	default y
	select WATCHDOG
	help
	  Sets up the SoC's watchdog on the watchdog0 devicetree alias and
	  feeds it from a work item on the system workqueue — but only while
	  every loop registered through mcuhome_health_liveness_register()
	  has checked in recently enough. A watchdog fed unconditionally by a
	  timer proves that the timer runs, which is never the thing that
	  broke; this one proves that the loops the device needs are running.

	  Missing one feed is harmless. Missing them for the whole timeout
	  resets the SoC, which is the only way an unreachable node comes
	  back — and, for an unconfirmed image, the way MCUboot gets to
	  revert it.

	  The alias is board wiring and is not optional: a board whose
	  devicetree has no watchdog0 fails the build with a message naming
	  the alias, rather than quietly shipping an image with no watchdog.
	  ADR 0015's health amendment makes this mandatory for every
	  application image.

config MCUHOME_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Watchdog timeout (seconds)"
	depends on MCUHOME_WATCHDOG
	default 60
	range 5 3600
	help
	  How long the device may go unfed before the SoC resets. Deliberately
	  generous: the feeder runs on the system workqueue, which the
	  networking stack shares, and a Matter node that is busy for several
	  seconds is doing its job, not hanging. The number that catches real
	  hangs is the per-slot silence budget, not this one — this is the
	  backstop for the feeder itself.

config MCUHOME_WATCHDOG_FEED_INTERVAL_S
	int "Watchdog feed interval (seconds)"
	depends on MCUHOME_WATCHDOG
	default 10
	range 1 600
	help
	  How often the feeder wakes up and re-checks the liveness slots. Must
	  be well below CONFIG_MCUHOME_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_S so that a single
	  late work item cannot reset the device; a build assert enforces at
	  least a factor of three.

config MCUHOME_WATCHDOG_LIVENESS_SLOTS
	int "Number of liveness slots"
	depends on MCUHOME_WATCHDOG
	default 4
	range 1 16
	help
	  Sizes the static slot table. One slot per loop that has to keep
	  running. The framework registers one, for the CHIP event loop; the
	  system workqueue needs none, because the feeder runs on it and a
	  feed IS the proof that it schedules. The rest of the table is for
	  components that come later.

config MCUHOME_CRASH_BREADCRUMB
	bool "Keep a crash breadcrumb across the reset and report it at boot"
	default y
	help
	  A fatal error reboots (CONFIG_MCUHOME_RESET_ON_FATAL_ERROR), which
	  is the right behaviour and also the reason nobody ever sees why:
	  the fault dump goes out over a log transport that, on a device in
	  the field, has no reader — and then the evidence is gone. This
	  keeps a nine-word record of the fault in uninitialized RAM, which
	  a soft reset does not clear, and logs it prominently on the next
	  boot, when a reader may well be attached.

	  What is kept: the reason code, PC and LR from the exception stack
	  frame, the SCB fault registers as far as they still hold anything
	  at that point, and a count of faults since the last power loss.
	  Roughly 36 bytes of RAM, a handful of stores in the fault handler
	  and one log line per boot.

	  The record is protected by a checksum rather than a magic word
	  alone, because the RAM it lives in holds junk after a power-up and
	  belongs to MCUboot before it belongs to the application. Losing a
	  report is acceptable; inventing one is not.

config MCUHOME_IMAGE_CONFIRM
	bool "Confirm the running image to MCUboot after a healthy delay"
	default y
	depends on MCUBOOT_IMG_MANAGER
	help
	  MCUboot's swap-with-revert scheme installs a new image as a *test*
	  image: unless something confirms it, the next boot swaps the
	  previous image back. That is the safety net under every over-the-air
	  update on a board with a staging slot, and it only works if a
	  healthy image actually confirms itself.

	  With this enabled the framework arms a timer when the device reaches
	  its operational point (mcuhome_health_operational(), called at the
	  end of Matter bring-up) and calls boot_write_img_confirmed() when it
	  fires — but only if the current boot really is a pending test image,
	  which boot_is_img_confirmed() answers. A normal boot of a confirmed
	  image writes nothing.

config MCUHOME_IMAGE_CONFIRM_DELAY_S
	int "Delay before confirming the running image (seconds)"
	depends on MCUHOME_IMAGE_CONFIRM
	default 30
	range 1 3600
	help
	  How long the new image has to stay up before it is trusted. Long
	  enough to cover bring-up crashes and an immediate watchdog reset;
	  short enough that a user watching an update finish does not have to
	  wait for it. ADR 0015's health amendment names ~30 s.

endif # MCUHOME_HEALTH
